def LAD(*a): return list(a)
def RUB(a,b,c,d): return [(a,'Missing or unclear','Meets the stated check',b),(c,'Guessing','Evidence kept',d),('Records','Gaps','Complete','Complete and reviewed by a peer'),('Safety and ethics','Overlooked','Followed','Followed and taught to others')]
COURSES = [
dict(slug='textiles', title='Textiles and Sustainable Manufacturing', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Follow fibre to yarn to fabric to garment, test quality with simple numbers, cut waste and water use, and run a small unit safely and legally.',
 outs=[('Identify','fibres and fabric structures by simple tests.'),('Apply','yarn count, fabric weight and cost calculations.'),('Analyse','defects and dye results to find the cause.'),('Evaluate','a unit for water, energy and worker safety.'),('Create','a costed production plan for one garment lot.')],
 task='Plan and track a small lot of 100 garments from fabric choice to packed goods, with a quality report and a sustainability record.', cond='About 12 weeks part-time. Fabric swatches, a hand lens, a weighing scale, a tape, access to a small stitching or weaving unit, a mentor.',
 standard='Fibres are identified correctly in 8 of 10 swatches; costs add up to within 2 percent; defects are logged per piece; water and waste per garment are measured and a reduction target is set.', who='Artisans, tailors, unit supervisors, design and merchandising students, co-operative members.', req='Basic arithmetic and ability to handle fabric and simple tools.',
 mods=[
 M('Fibres and their properties','Identify',5,['Sort fibres into natural plant (cotton, jute), natural animal (wool, silk) and man-made (polyester, viscose).','Use the burn test with tongs in a safe tray: cotton burns fast with paper smell, wool curls with hair smell, polyester melts into a hard bead.','Check feel, strength and moisture take-up under a hand lens.','Match fibre to use: cotton for comfort, polyester for strength and quick drying.'],'Ten swatches are numbered; a learner burns a 2 cm thread of each and names 8 correctly, noting that swatch 4 melted and so is polyester.','Identify ten fibre swatches and record the evidence.','At least 8 of 10 are named correctly with the test result written.','Why is the burn test done in a tray with tongs?','To avoid burns and fire and to catch melted drips.'),
 M('Yarn making and yarn count','Apply',5,['Follow the steps: cleaning, carding, drawing, spinning, winding.','Weigh a 1 metre length of yarn to find its thickness.','Use the direct system: tex equals grams per 1000 metres.','Check twist, because too little twist weakens yarn and too much makes it kinky.'],'A 10 metre piece of yarn weighs 0.6 g, so 1000 metres weigh 60 g and the yarn is 60 tex.','Weigh three yarns and calculate tex.','All three tex values are correct and the thickest yarn is named.','What does tex measure?','Grams of yarn per 1000 metres, so higher tex is thicker.'),
 M('Weaving, knitting and fabric structure','Analyse',6,['Tell woven fabric (two thread sets crossing) from knitted fabric (loops).','Name the plain, twill and satin weaves by their float pattern.','Count threads per inch in warp and weft with a pick glass.','Choose structure for use: knits stretch, woven fabrics hold shape.'],'A shirting fabric has 60 warp and 52 weft threads per inch, giving 112 threads per square inch, which is a medium-dense plain weave.','Analyse five fabrics for structure and thread count.','Structure and count are recorded and a use is justified for each.','Why do knitted fabrics stretch more than woven ones?','Loops can open and change shape, while crossed threads lock.'),
 M('Dyeing, printing and finishing','Apply',6,['Prepare fabric by scouring so dye takes evenly.','Follow a recipe by liquor ratio, for example 1 kg fabric in 20 litres of water.','Wear gloves, apron and mask, and keep dyes away from food areas.','Fix colour, rinse until water runs clear, and test for rubbing and wash fastness.'],'A 2 kg lot at a 1 to 20 ratio needs 40 litres; the dye is 2 percent of fabric weight, which is 40 g.','Dye a 200 g swatch lot and run a wash test.','Colour is even, quantities are calculated correctly and fastness is graded.','How much water does 3 kg of fabric need at a 1 to 20 ratio?','60 litres.'),
 M('Pattern, cutting and stitching','Create',6,['Take body measures in centimetres and record them.','Draft a basic pattern with seam allowance of 1.5 cm.','Plan the cutting layout to waste less fabric.','Set stitch length and tension on a test piece before the lot.'],'A kurta needs 2.4 metres in a good layout; a tighter layout brings it to 2.2 metres, saving 0.2 metres on each of 100 pieces, which is 20 metres.','Draft, cut and stitch one garment.','Seams are straight and measures match within 1 cm.','Why test stitching on scrap first?','To set tension and length without ruining the garment.'),
 M('Quality control and defects','Analyse',5,['Inspect each piece for holes, stains, loose threads and uneven seams.','Count defects per 100 pieces and chart them.','Trace the cause: needle damage, wrong tension, dirty machine.','Fix the cause and re-check the next batch.'],'In 100 shirts, 12 had skipped stitches; changing a blunt needle cut the next lot to 3, a drop of 75 percent.','Inspect a lot of 20 pieces and log defects.','Every defect is logged with a cause and a fix.','Why trace the cause rather than just repair the piece?','So the defect does not repeat in the next lot.'),
 M('Sustainability, safety and law','Evaluate',5,['Measure water used per kilogram of fabric and set a cut target.','Treat dye water and reuse rinse water where possible; do not dump effluent in drains or fields.','Keep fire exits clear, wire safe, and lint cleaned; provide guards and seating for workers.','Follow the labour codes on wages and hours and the law on child and adolescent labour, register the unit (for example on Udyam), and check State Pollution Control Board consent where dyeing is done.'],'A unit using 120 litres per kilogram reuses the final rinse and falls to 90 litres, saving 30 litres per kilogram or 25 percent.','Audit a unit with a checklist.','The audit lists five risks, each with an action and an owner.','Why is untreated dye water a problem?','It pollutes water and soil and harms health and farming.'),
 M('Capstone: a costed lot of 100 garments','Create',6,['Choose fabric, count yardage and add 5 percent for waste.','Cost fabric, trims, labour, power and packing per piece.','Run the lot with a quality log and a water record.','Set a price with a margin and a plan to reduce waste.'],'Cost per piece: fabric 180, trims 20, labour 90, power 10, packing 10, total 310 rupees; with a 20 percent margin the price is 372 rupees.','Present the plan, log and price.','Costs reconcile within 2 percent and the quality and water records are complete.','What proves the lot was well planned?','Costs, quality log and waste data that match the plan.')],
 extra=[('Why add an allowance to fabric yardage?','Cutting and faults waste some cloth.'),('Why keep a defect log?','To find causes and prevent repeats.')],
 rubric=RUB('Technical accuracy','Correct identification, counts and costs with sources','Quality','Defects logged, traced and reduced'),
 ladder=LAD('Know common fibres and fabrics.','Explain the fibre to garment chain.','Do tests and costing under guidance.','Run a small lot alone.','Supervise a line and manage quality.','Manage a unit with sustainability targets.','Lead standards and training across a cluster or sector.'),
 jobs=['Textile technologist','Quality inspector','Fashion designer','Merchandiser','Garment unit supervisor']),

dict(slug='biotechnology', title='Biotechnology Foundations', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Learn cell biology, safe lab practice, fermentation and basic data work, and judge ethics and safety rules, using small experiments and real calculations.',
 outs=[('Describe','cell structures and the flow from gene to protein.'),('Apply','lab safety, measurement and sterile technique.'),('Analyse','growth and fermentation data.'),('Evaluate','a biotech use against ethics and biosafety rules.'),('Create','a small experiment report with a method, data and conclusion.')],
 task='Run one small fermentation or growth experiment, record data, analyse it, and write a short report with a safety and ethics note.', cond='About 12 weeks part-time. A school or college lab or a safe kitchen lab, a microscope, a balance, a thermometer, a mentor.',
 standard='Measurements are within 5 percent of target, controls are used, data is graphed, and the report states the result, the limits and the safety steps followed.', who='Science graduates, lab assistants, agriculture and food students, teachers, early researchers.', req='School-level biology and chemistry, and basic arithmetic.',
 mods=[
 M('Cells and microscopes','Describe',5,['Name the parts: wall, membrane, nucleus, mitochondria, chloroplast.','Tell plant, animal and bacterial cells apart.','Focus a microscope from low to high power and never use the coarse knob at high power.','Draw what you see with a scale.'],'Under 400x magnification an onion cell appears 4 mm long, so its real length is 4 mm divided by 400, which is 0.01 mm.','Prepare and draw two slides with scale.','Drawings are labelled and sizes are calculated correctly.','How do you find real size from a drawing?','Divide the drawn size by the magnification.'),
 M('Lab safety and biosafety','Apply',5,['Wear coat, gloves and eye cover, and tie back hair.','Treat all cultures as harmful: no eating, no mouth pipetting, wash hands after work.','Disinfect benches before and after, and kill waste cultures by autoclave or a 1 percent bleach soak for 30 minutes.','Know your institution\'s rules and keep records of each biological material used.'],'A 1 percent bleach solution is 10 ml of household bleach made up to 1 litre with water.','Complete a safety checklist and a spill drill.','Checklist is complete and the spill steps are done in order.','How long does waste soak in 1 percent bleach?','At least 30 minutes.'),
 M('Measurement, solutions and dilution','Apply',6,['Weigh with a balance and record to two decimals.','Make a percent solution: grams of solute per 100 ml.','Use C1 V1 equals C2 V2 for dilution.','Label every tube with contents, date and your initials.'],'To make 200 ml of 0.5 percent salt solution, weigh 1.0 g of salt and add water to 200 ml.','Make three solutions and one dilution series.','Concentrations are within 5 percent of target.','How much salt is in 500 ml of 2 percent solution?','10 g.'),
 M('DNA, genes and proteins','Describe',5,['Explain DNA as a code of four bases that is copied and read.','Follow the path from gene to messenger RNA to protein.','Read a simple codon table.','Explain how a change in one base can change a protein.'],'The codons AUG, GGC, UUU read as methionine, glycine, phenylalanine; three bases make one amino acid.','Translate a 12 base sequence to amino acids.','The four amino acids are correct.','How many bases code for one amino acid?','Three.'),
 M('Sterile technique and culture','Apply',6,['Work near a flame or in a clean cabinet and sterilise tools.','Prepare growth medium and sterilise it at 121 degrees Celsius for 15 minutes in a pressure steriliser or pressure cooker under supervision.','Streak a plate to get single colonies.','Incubate at the right temperature and seal plates before opening for counts.'],'A plate streaked with yeast at 30 degrees Celsius shows 40 separate colonies after 48 hours.','Streak and incubate three plates and one blank control.','Control stays clean and colonies are separated.','Why keep a blank control plate?','To show that contamination did not come from the method.'),
 M('Fermentation and bioprocess','Analyse',6,['Set up yeast in a sugar solution with a balloon or gas trap.','Change one factor: temperature, sugar or time.','Measure gas volume or sugar left at fixed times.','Plot the curve and find the fastest period.'],'Three flasks at 25, 30 and 37 degrees Celsius make 30, 52 and 38 ml of gas in an hour; 30 degrees Celsius works best.','Run the experiment and graph results.','Graph is correct and best condition is justified with numbers.','Why change only one factor at a time?','So the cause of any difference is clear.'),
 M('Bioinformatics and data basics','Analyse',5,['Store data in a table with units.','Compute mean and spread from three repeats.','Compare two DNA sequences by counting matching bases.','Use public sequence databases and keep a record of what was searched.'],'Two 20 base sequences match in 17 places, so identity is 17 divided by 20, which is 85 percent.','Compute identity for three sequence pairs.','All percentages are correct.','What does 85 percent identity mean?','85 of every 100 bases match.'),
 M('Ethics, rules and the final report','Create',5,['Check national biosafety and animal ethics rules before any work with gene change, animals or human samples.','Ask consent and protect privacy for any human data.','Write a report: aim, method, data, graph, result, limits, safety.','Share results honestly, including failed runs.'],'A report states: best temperature 30 degrees Celsius, mean gas 52 ml over three repeats, limit of one yeast strain only.','Present the report with an ethics note.','Report includes a graph, limits and ethical steps.','Why report failed runs?','They show the true method limits and prevent repeated errors.')],
 extra=[('Why use repeats in an experiment?','To check the result is consistent and not chance.'),('Why get consent for human samples?','It respects the person and is a legal and ethical duty.')],
 rubric=RUB('Method','Controlled, repeated and clearly written','Data analysis','Correct calculations and graphs with limits stated'),
 ladder=LAD('Know basic cell and gene ideas.','Explain how cultures grow.','Follow a protocol under guidance.','Design and run a small experiment.','Lead a lab team and train others.','Manage a lab quality and biosafety system.','Set standards or lead research programmes.'),
 jobs=['Lab technician','Research assistant','Quality control analyst','Fermentation technician','Biosafety assistant']),

dict(slug='minerals-and-resource-management', title='Minerals and Resource Management', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Identify minerals, read maps and simple grades, plan safe and legal extraction, process ore, and restore land with community care.',
 outs=[('Identify','common minerals by simple tests.'),('Apply','grade, tonnage and recovery calculations.'),('Analyse','a site for safety and environmental risk.'),('Evaluate','legal permissions and community effects.'),('Create','a closure and land restoration plan.')],
 task='Study a sample site or case, estimate its ore, plan safe working and write a restoration plan with legal and community steps.', cond='About 12 weeks part-time. Mineral samples, a hardness kit, a map and a compass or phone mapping, a scale, a mentor.',
 standard='Eight of ten minerals are identified correctly; tonnage and grade calculations are correct; the risk register covers dust, water, slope and noise; the closure plan has dated actions.', who='Geology and mining students, surveyors, supervisors, environment officers, small recovery operators.', req='Basic arithmetic and fitness for outdoor field work.',
 mods=[
 M('Minerals and rocks','Identify',5,['Sort rocks into igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic.','Test colour, streak, shine and cleavage.','Use the Mohs scale: a nail scratches minerals up to hardness 2.5 and a steel knife up to about 5.5.','Record each sample with a number and place.'],'A grey mineral with a black streak that a nail cannot scratch but a knife can is hardness between 2.5 and 5.5.','Identify ten samples with the test results.','At least 8 of 10 are named with evidence.','What does streak show?','The colour of the powder, which is more reliable than surface colour.'),
 M('Maps, location and sampling','Apply',6,['Read a topographic map with contours and scale.','Mark sample points by grid or phone location and record them.','Collect samples from set spacing, for example every 50 metres.','Label bags and keep a chain of custody log.'],'A 500 metre line sampled every 50 metres gives 11 samples including both ends.','Plan and mark a sampling line on a map.','Spacing and sample count are correct and labelled.','Why record the exact point of each sample?','Results can then be traced to place on a map.'),
 M('Grade, tonnage and value','Apply',6,['Grade is the percent of the useful mineral in the rock.','Tonnage equals volume times density.','Metal in ore equals tonnage times grade.','Use cut-off grade, the lowest grade worth mining.'],'An ore body of 10000 cubic metres at density 2.7 is 27000 tonnes; at 1.5 percent grade it holds 405 tonnes of metal.','Calculate tonnage and metal for three blocks.','All calculations are correct with units.','How much metal is in 2000 tonnes at 2 percent?','40 tonnes.'),
 M('Safe and legal extraction','Evaluate',6,['Check that a lease, environmental clearance and permissions are in place before work.','Wear helmet, boots, dust mask, ear defenders and high visibility clothing.','Keep slopes and benches at the designed angle and inspect after rain.','Never work alone near edges, blasting or machines; follow the licensed blaster rules.'],'A bench 6 metres high needs at least a 6 metre wide working floor; the slope after rain is inspected before any work restarts.','Review a site against a legal and safety checklist.','Every item is marked met or not met with an action.','Why inspect slopes after rain?','Water weakens ground and can cause collapse.'),
 M('Processing and recovery','Apply',6,['Crush and grind ore to free the mineral.','Separate by size, gravity or other method suited to the ore.','Calculate recovery as metal in product divided by metal in feed.','Keep tailings in a lined and managed area.'],'Feed of 100 tonnes at 2 percent holds 2 tonnes of metal; the concentrate holds 1.7 tonnes, so recovery is 85 percent.','Calculate recovery for two plant runs.','Recovery values are correct and a loss point is named.','What is recovery?','The share of metal in the feed that ends up in the product.'),
 M('Environment, water and dust','Analyse',5,['List risks: dust, noise, water use, acid drainage, loss of habitat.','Measure water used per tonne and set a reuse target.','Use sprinklers, covers and green belts to control dust.','Test nearby water before work, during work and after, and keep the results.'],'A plant using 1.5 cubic metres of water per tonne reuses 40 percent, so fresh use falls to 0.9 cubic metres per tonne.','Build a risk register for a site.','Register covers five risks with controls and owners.','Why test water before work starts?','It gives a baseline to show any later change.'),
 M('Community, rights and circular recovery','Evaluate',5,['Hold meetings with local people and record concerns and replies.','Respect land rights, local employment and fair pay.','Recover metal from scrap and old equipment to cut new mining.','Report production and payments to the authority honestly.'],'A scrap yard recovering 3 tonnes of copper cable a month avoids mining about 300 tonnes of ore at 1 percent grade.','Prepare a community consultation record.','Concerns, replies and commitments are written and dated.','Why record community concerns?','They show promises made and let you check they were kept.'),
 M('Capstone: site study and closure plan','Create',6,['Summarise samples, tonnage and recovery.','State permits needed and safety controls.','Plan closure: reshape slopes, return topsoil, plant native trees.','Set dated actions, costs and a five-year monitoring plan.'],'Closure of 2 hectares: 15 cm topsoil layer, 2000 saplings at 1 per 10 square metres, monitoring twice a year for five years.','Present the study and the plan.','Plan has dated steps, costs and monitoring that tie to the risks.','What shows that restoration worked?','Stable slopes, living plant cover and clean water results.')],
 extra=[('Why keep a chain of custody for samples?','It proves where each sample came from and that it was not changed.'),('What is cut-off grade?','The lowest grade that still pays to mine.')],
 rubric=RUB('Calculations','Correct with units and checks','Compliance','Permits, safety and community steps all evidenced'),
 ladder=LAD('Know common minerals and terms.','Explain how ore becomes metal.','Sample and calculate under guidance.','Plan a small site alone.','Supervise safety and operations.','Manage a site with environmental targets.','Set policy and review sites across a region.'),
 jobs=['Mineral surveyor','Mine safety supervisor','Process technician','Environment officer','Recycling and recovery operator']),

dict(slug='waste-management', title='Waste Management and Circular Resources', level='L4 Practitioner',
 summary='Audit, segregate, collect, process and report waste for homes, towns and industry, following the law, protecting workers and turning waste into value.',
 outs=[('Classify','waste into the legal streams.'),('Apply','weights, volumes and route calculations.'),('Analyse','a waste audit to find reduction chances.'),('Evaluate','a plan for safety, law and cost.'),('Create','a waste plan with targets and a monthly report.')],
 task='Audit the waste of one site, design a segregation and collection plan, run it for a month and report results against targets.', cond='About 12 weeks part-time. A housing society, shop, school or small factory, bins, a hanging scale, gloves, masks, a mentor.',
 standard='Waste is split into correct streams; audit weights add up within 3 percent; diversion from landfill rises by a set target; workers have protective gear and training.', who='Municipal staff, supervisors, society managers, recycling entrepreneurs, self-help group leaders, sustainability officers.', req='Basic arithmetic and willingness to work with practical field tasks.',
 mods=[
 M('Waste streams and the law','Classify',5,['Know the main streams: wet, dry recyclable, sanitary and hazardous, plus e-waste, plastic, construction and biomedical.','Use the colour code your city follows, usually green for wet and blue for dry.','Know that bulk generators must segregate at source and follow the national solid waste rules.','Keep hazardous waste away from household streams.'],'A shop throws away vegetable peel, cardboard, used batteries and a broken tube light; they go to wet, dry, hazardous (battery) and e-waste (lamp) respectively.','Classify 20 items into correct streams.','At least 18 of 20 are correct.','Where does a used battery go?','To hazardous or e-waste collection, never to household bins.'),
 M('Waste audit','Analyse',6,['Collect one full day of waste from the site.','Sort it by stream and weigh each part with the scale.','Calculate each stream as a percent of the total.','Repeat for three days and average.'],'Daily waste is 80 kg: wet 44 kg, dry recyclable 28 kg, other 8 kg, so wet is 55 percent.','Audit one site for three days.','Stream weights add up to the total within 3 percent.','How do you find percent of a stream?','Stream weight divided by total weight times 100.'),
 M('Segregation at source and behaviour','Apply',5,['Place bins in the right spots with clear colour and picture labels.','Train residents or staff in a 15 minute session.','Give feedback on mistakes and thank good practice.','Check bins weekly for contamination and report the rate.'],'A society finds 18 percent of dry bins contain food; after two weeks of training it falls to 6 percent.','Run a short training and check bins.','Contamination rate is measured before and after.','Why use pictures on bin labels?','They help every reader, including those who cannot read the language.'),
 M('Composting and wet waste','Apply',6,['Mix two parts brown dry leaves with one part green kitchen waste.','Keep the pile moist like a squeezed sponge and turn it weekly.','Expect compost in 6 to 8 weeks; the finished pile smells earthy.','Fix problems: smell means too wet, no heat means too dry or too little green.'],'A society with 44 kg wet waste a day makes about 11 kg of compost a day, since compost is about a quarter of the input weight.','Set up a small compost pit and log temperature.','Pile reaches warmth within a week and has no foul smell.','What does a foul smell in compost show?','Too wet or too little air, so add dry material and turn it.'),
 M('Collection, routes and recycling','Apply',6,['Plan a route that visits dense areas first and avoids backtracking.','Calculate trips from vehicle capacity and daily waste.','Sell clean dry recyclables to registered buyers and keep weight slips.','Use a transfer point for sorting before final disposal.'],'A 1.5 tonne vehicle must collect 6 tonnes a day, so it needs 4 trips.','Plan a route for 200 homes.','Trips and times are calculated and the route is mapped.','How many trips for 9 tonnes with a 1.5 tonne vehicle?','Six trips.'),
 M('Worker safety and health','Evaluate',5,['Provide gloves, boots, masks, reflective vests and hand wash points.','Offer vaccines and regular health checks.','Never let workers sort mixed waste with bare hands or enter pits without clearance.','Keep an incident log and first aid kit.'],'A team of 12 gets 12 sets of gloves, boots and masks and a monthly health camp; the log shows 2 minor cuts, both treated.','Write a safety plan and check gear.','Plan lists gear, training and emergency steps.','Why ban bare-hand sorting?','Sharp items and germs can cause injury and infection.'),
 M('Hazardous, e-waste and plastics','Evaluate',5,['Store e-waste and batteries in dry closed containers.','Hand them only to authorised collectors and keep receipts.','Know that producers have take-back duties for plastics and electronics.','Never burn waste, because smoke harms health and is illegal.'],'A school collects 40 kg of e-waste in a term and hands it to an authorised recycler against a signed receipt.','Set up a collection point and a record sheet.','Each handover has a receipt and the log matches.','Why keep receipts for e-waste handover?','They prove it reached an authorised recycler.'),
 M('Capstone: site waste plan','Create',6,['Set targets, for example diversion from landfill from 30 percent to 70 percent.','Run the plan for a month with daily weights.','Cost it: bins, labour, transport, revenue from recyclables and compost.','Write a monthly report with charts and next steps.'],'Waste of 2,400 kg a month; 1,700 kg diverted is 71 percent; costs Rs 9,000 against Rs 4,200 of sales, net cost Rs 4,800.','Present the plan and report.','Report shows weights, diversion, costs and corrective actions.','What proves the plan worked?','Higher diversion with lower contamination and complete records.')],
 extra=[('Why weigh waste instead of guessing?','Numbers show what to reduce and prove improvement.'),('Why not burn waste?','Smoke harms health and open burning is against the rules.')],
 rubric=RUB('Audit accuracy','Weights, percents and targets correct','Compliance','Legal streams, receipts and safety steps all evidenced'),
 ladder=LAD('Know the waste streams.','Explain segregation and why it matters.','Audit a site under guidance.','Run a site plan alone.','Supervise a team and routes.','Manage a ward or large facility with targets.','Lead city or sector waste systems and policy.'),
 jobs=['Waste management officer','Sanitation supervisor','Recycling entrepreneur','Compost unit operator','Environment health and safety assistant']),
]
